   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.3. Increased capacity of member States to set adequate wages and promote decent working time</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Empirical Framework for Setting Domestic Workers&apos; Minimum Wages jointly developed by National Wages and Productivity Commission (NWPC) and Philippine Statistical and Research Training Institute (PSRTI) was approved by the Commission en Banc on 14 September 2021. Both the framework and operational guidelines will strengthen the consultation process through the use of evidence-base information, guiding the Regional Tripartite Wages and Productivity Boards for setting minimum wage under the Republic Act No. 10361 and the Domestic Workers Law.
---
On January 18, 2021, the House of Representatives (HOR) adopted a bill &quot;An Act Providing Enhanced Protection Security, Security and Benefits for Media Workers&quot;, which was subsequently submitted to the Senate (Upper House) on January 10, 2021 (as the next step of the legislative process). This bill address working conditions (including working hours) specific to the context of this industry. In 2019, the ILO provided technical and financial support to the National Wages and Productivity Commission (NWPC) in the development of the Labor Force Survey rider module in October 2019 to assess employment and working conditions of domestic workers in the regions. The results of the rider module were finalized and presented to the Commission, Wage Boards and tripartite-plus partners in November 2020. 

Other ILO support included the conduct of the enumerators training and the pre-testing, as well as the conduct of consultations and the Data Dissemination Forum on the survey results.

The results of the rider module served as an input to the Empirical Framework for Setting Domestic Workers&apos; Minimum Wages. 
-----
The ILO participated and provided technical inputs in the series of discussions at the House Committee of Labor and Employment (July &#x2013; September 2020)</narrative>
   </description>
